 a really  i don't know it's just like a really  immersive experience or at least i found  it that  now if you continue listening to this  video past this sentence some plot  points might be revealed that you don't  want to know it's really really hard to  talk about this book without talking  about some of the stuff that happens in  it i'm not gonna spoil the ending or say  what you know what exactly is going on  and stuff like that but to talk about it  i am gonna have to like reveal some plot  points and as i said i would just  suggest going into this not knowing  anything about it like i did so if  you're not gonna read it uh you know or  if you've already read it and you wanna  go ahead and listen then you can but i'm  just like warning you right now that you  know it's better going into it if you  don't know anything about it and i'm  gonna tell you a little bit about it so  i don't know if i'd actually go so far  as to call the book meta  but it does actually start with the  author have that she's like a forward  and she is describing her own  experiences with night terrors and she  says how a psychologist told her that  one thing that she could do was like set  an alarm for the middle of the night so  that when she has a night terror and the  alarm wakes her up she needs to  immediately write it down  because he's saying well if you keep  doing that then it kind of like takes  their power away  so she says this is where the germ of  the idea for the bell chime came from it  was one of these night terrors that she  woke up in the middle of the night and  like wrote down and actually in the  afterwords she  has like her notes there like what  exactly what had happened in the dream  that she woke up and that she wrote it  down so she does actually tell you at  the end like exactly what it was and  like why it gave her an idea for this  for this story so when we get into the  story proper we're following uh an  initially unnamed woman  now she wakes up at the beginning and  she seems to be  encased in some kind of bag like almost  like a body bag or something like that  and she's laying on a cold metal table  and there's somebody hovering over her  who sort of looks like their head is  deformed  and they're asking her repeatedly if she  can hear the bell chime  now when she says that she can i mean  she's obviously like terrified and it's  really hot she's sweating inside this  bag and everything like that she says  she can hear it and then she's suddenly  subjected to like excruciating pain like  it's like it feels like somebody is kind  of like  burying their arms like into her  ribs and like pulling her ribs apart and  stuff like that like horrible horrible  pain so then she wakes up from what was  presumably a nightmare  she's in her apartment in manhattan and  there's her concerned uh and very very  attractive muscle-bound uh kind of  perfect boyfriend dexter is there  comforting her  so we learned that the protagonist is a  very successful novelist uh in the term  there's kind of like a throwaway thing  later where she gets a phone call and  it's like you know some book deals like  two million dollars something like that  so you know what i mean so so she's like  very successful novelist and her  boyfriend like i said is kind of  seemingly perfect you know he's  beautiful looking it's like he always  smells like roses he's you know he's  totally on her wavelength and knows what  she's thinking all the time and stuff  like that  um so she's got all that going on  but she's also plagued uh quite often by  these terrible nightmares and maybe  we're led to believe is a little bit  mentally fragile because of some  unspoken issues or something that  happened in the past so when she wakes  up it's sunday morning and dexter is  making breakfast but they're out of  bacon you know can't have that so the  protagonist whose name we actually don't  learn until later but is actually lauren  so that's what i'm gonna call her from  now on even though you don't find that  out until later in the book she says oh  i'll just pop down to the little grocery  store across the street to get some  on her way out of the building though  she sees this flyer that somebody has  posted like on the window glass of the  door like from the building like out  into the street  it's a missing person's flyer but the  photo on it is of her but not quite like  it is her  but in the photo she looks like insane  or like demonic  and she's like i never posed for that  picture so where did whoever makes like  where did they get that and she's like i  wouldn't even be able to make my face  look like that if i tried to so she's  like really really unnerved about it  like i said i love stories that start  out with weird [ __ ] like that i mean  that's like such a great  intriguing premise that you're just kind  of like what the [ __ ] like where is this  gonna go like from now on  so she's obviously very freaked out and  she's also really mad because she thinks  somebody's like [ __ ] with her right  so she takes the flyer back into the  apartment and shows dexter her boyfriend  now he doesn't take it all that  seriously um but he does come up with  the somewhat reasonable explanation that  maybe a weird fan of hers or a hater um  maybe put it there as a prank like they  photoshopped a picture of her and and  then put it there like to get under her  skin because like i said in you know in  the context of the story she's  supposedly this big famous writer so she  has like a big fan base so he's like  maybe it was just somebody like that  just to try to like [ __ ] with you so  lauren is somewhat mollified by this but  she's like still pretty unnerved by the  fact she's like look i didn't pose for  that picture and yeah i know photoshop  is a thing but  you know uh that's me and i don't know  how they would have photoshopped me to  like look like that and as the story is  going on she also starts to suspect that  maybe her  you were led to believe her delicate  hold on sanity might be crumbling once  again because it's she says or she  thinks things to the effect of like it's  happening again or something like that  and this suspicion is heightened by the  fact that she's sure that the eyes of  the photo are like moving and like  following her around like they're  watching her  um and she also like later on she throws  the flyer away at one point because it's  freaking her out but it somehow returns  like kind of gets itself out of the  garbage and stuff and what's worse  another one appears where the photo of  her  looks um it's her but it's it looks like  horribly horribly like unutterably sad  like not demonic like the other one and  from that point on lauren starts to hear  this bell chime invading her waking life  like from her dream like can you hear  the bell chime and she starts to see  even more terrible things that may or  may not be hallucinations  at about a quarter of the way through  the story  lauren wakes up inside the bag and on  the metal table from her dream again and  the actual truth of her situation like  starts to become much clearer from there  on out  now as i mentioned the bell chime  jumps back and forth in the timeline and  skates between reality and nightmares  but in spite of all that it remains  completely lucid and very very simple to  follow the entire time i had zero  trouble following what was going on or  anything like that it's very i mean the  clarity is just like is really really  good  so new information gets revealed with  every section and every sort of journey  into the past and into the dream world  and it all in the end it just all comes  together really really seamlessly like  you really get a sense of like what was  happening  now i have seen a couple of reviewers  that compared this story to maybe like  something you'd see on black mirror  and that's a pretty fair comparison i'd  say although  the bell chime is actually set in the  present day and it doesn't really have  any science fiction trappings or any  particular focus on technology so like  in that way it's sort of not like that  but this is really just  purely psychological horror at its  finest and at its most real too i mean  you can really tell just by reading this  that the author has probably experienced  uh many of the frightening emotions and  visions that she describes even if it  was only in her dreams like it comes  across as like very genuine  so the overarching theme of the story  which is very succinctly summed up in  the book's last sentence is what would  you abandon for happiness  and that is definitely a question that  you will probably be asking yourself for  quite a while after you have finished  reading this book this is definitely one  of those stories that is going to stick  with you and is going to like haunt your  thoughts from time to time like as you  kind of go about your life  and i mean the imagery that she was able  to conjure up through her use of  language is like like so vivid and it's  just like really all-encompassing like  all her use of metaphor and some of it's  just like gorgeous  and the way she conveys like the mood  and the emotion i mean just like really  incredible i mean just the way like i  said it's just beautifully beautifully  written it really is very very evocative  so i'm gonna say like uh mona kabani  she's undoubtedly gonna be a writer to  watch in coming years and i'm actually  really excited to read more of her stuff  because this was just exquisite i mean  this was really really good and as far  as i know i think she's written at least  two more novels maybe three uh one of  them is called vanilla a lot of people  had uh uh recommended that one like i  said that was really good so i will  eventually like get around to reading  that as well because i really really  like this a lot i mean it's creepy it's  beautifully written and it was just such  a unique  story that i mean you know it